course: dictionary
lesson: 8540
title: Say: one small word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Say is a small word with many jobs. Most of the time it means to speak words: «Say hello.» It can also mean to tell a fact or opinion, as in «The sign says stop», where the sign is doing the telling. The words around say give you the clues to which meaning is right.
explain: Say can also mean «for example» or «let's imagine». In «Take a number, say 7», the writer wants you to pick 7 as an example. Your say is your chance to give your opinion: «Everyone gets a say» means everyone is allowed to share what they think.
explain: Say changes its shape: says, said, saying. When you tell what someone said, you use reported speech, as in «she says that…». Say is also a casual word for surprise. «I say» and «you don't say» show surprise, and «you don't say» can be sarcastic, meaning you already knew that.
explain: Formal writing uses stiffer words instead of say, such as state, declare and indicate. «The notice indicates the road is closed» means the same as «the notice says the road is closed». Writers pick these words to sound serious and official.
fact: Say means to speak words, or to tell a fact or opinion.
fact: «Say» can mean «for example», as in «take a number, say 7».
fact: Your say is your chance to give your opinion.
fact: «Says who?» is a casual challenge: it asks who decided that.
fact: Formal writing uses words like state, declare and indicate instead of say.
exercise: «"You can't get burnt when it's cloudy," says your big sister.» What is the sister doing? | Eating her lunch | Running away | Singing a song | *Speaking words to tell you a fact
exercise: «Take a number, say 7.» What does say mean here? | *For example | Speak loudly | Stop counting | Write it down
exercise: Joe asks, «Says who?» What is he doing? | Saying thank you | *Challenging someone: asking who decided that | Agreeing with a friend | Telling a fact about the sun
test: «Everyone gets a say» in the class vote. What is a say? | *Your chance to give your opinion | A spelling mistake | A loud shout | A type of game
test: Which word is a stiffer, formal word for say? | Bounce | *Declare | Shout | Giggle
exam: «If Gran says "keep the change", telling her how much is left makes it an honest gift.» What is Gran doing when she says «keep the change»? | Counting coins in a jar | Writing a letter to the bank | Buying a new coat | *Speaking words to tell you what she wants
